    "body": "Great on any Friday: RTIC Ultra-Tough 30 Can Soft Cooler\nOfficial LEGO New Glenn Rocket and Jacklyn Landing Barge Set Is Not Sold by LEGO\n12 photos\nPhoto: Blue Origin\nTry as I might, I can't remember if I ever came across an official LEGO set that's not distributed by LEGO itself. Yet this is exactly what happens with the Blue Origin New Glenn and Jacklyn set announced this week by Jeff Bezos' space company.\nThe toy is rather small, as it only needs 550 bricks to come together and form something that looks like the company's star rocket and the barge that was built as a sort of offshore landing pad.\nWhen completed, the set stands 10.5 inches (27 cm) tall, and it seems to detail the two bits of hardware as best it can. By the looks of it, only the New Glenn's two stages are represented, with the fairing missing completely from the picture. The Jacklyn, on the other hand, seems to be complete.\nThe set is advertised as a limited edition, but we're not told whether it's limited in time or production numbers. You can buy yourself one only on the Blue Origin website by paying $294.95 for it. The waiting time is quite short, with the estimates saying deliveries should begin as soon as the week of November 30.\nIn real life, the New Glenn rocket that's replicated in LEGO bricks is a two-stage launch vehicle that's built in two versions at the moment, 7x2 and 9x4 – the numbers represent the number of engines fitted on the first and second stages, respectively. A third stage, called GS3, has been announced earlier this month.\nWhy would anyone want to have a New Glenn LEGO set in their home? Well, one reason would be the fact that this piece of hardware will be heavily involved in future space exploration missions of unprecedented scope and scale.\nIn its 7x2 incarnation, the New Glenn has been flown three times (not always successfully): first with the Blue Ring Pathfinder demonstrator, then to launch NASA's Escape and Plasma Acceleration and Dynamics Explorers (ESCAPADE) mission to Mars, and finally with a BlueBird 7 satellite on board.\nThe 9x4, on the other hand, hasn't been flown yet, but it will probably get to work pretty soon. That's because Blue Origin will have to launch a lot of missions in the coming years. It will start next year with the launch of the Blue Moon lander in support of the NASA Artemis program, as well as the Volatiles Investigating Polar Exploration Rover (VIPER) that'll look for water and other volatiles on the Moon.\nThen, in 2028, Blue Origin will have to send one or both lunar terrain vehicles (LTV, made by private contractors Lunar Outpost and Astrolab) to the Moon in support of the Artemis landings.\nIn the foreseeable future, both versions of the New Glenn will be used in parallel, depending on the mission ahead. Stick the landing... one brick at a time! The limited edition New Glenn & Jacklyn LEGO set just touched down, and pre-orders are OPEN! Build the landing platform. Stack the booster. Land the booster right on the deck and bring it to port on your favorite display. Pre-order today and bring Blue Origin home, piece by piece. https://t.co/CGyf0a7YF7 — Blue Origin (@blueorigin) November 25, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: Gallery shows the real New Glenn.\nblue origin new glenn Lego\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Turbo Honda Civic Drags a Dodge Charger SRT Hellcat, and Someone Gets Thrashed After a Little Nap",
